Setting, from p.49 and this page: V is a 2-dimensional vector space (complex, possibly with a real structure). Σ = P(V) is the projective line of lines F ⊂ V. O_Σ(1) has fibre O_F(1) = V/F at the point x = F. PΣ is the associated principal C*-bundle. ω is the orientation torsor of V, and R(ω) = R ∧^{±1} ω. Exterior product gives F ⊗ V/F ≅ det V ≅ R(ω), hence O_F(1) ≅ F̌(ω). A nondegenerate quadratic form e on V identifies this with F^⊥.

On p.49 this gives two descriptions of the fibre of PΣ:
- (19): (PΣ)_F ≅ (V/F)* ≅ (F^⊥)*;
- (20): (PΣ)_F ≅ F*(ω) ≅ (F^⊥)*.

This page first notes that the identification behind (20) reverses an orientation. Varying F, the two descriptions give two global isomorphisms of C*-torsors:
- (21) PΣ ≅ V(ω)* (non-zero vectors of V(ω), or of its dual);
- PΣ ≅ V*.

Grothendieck then compares them via canonical linear maps:
- exterior product (dim V = 2) gives V ≅ V̌ ⊗ det V = V̌(ω);
- the quadratic form e gives V ≅ V̌, hence V̌(ω) ≅ V(ω);
- composing yields a canonical isomorphism V ≅ V(ω).

This induces a map σ: Σ = P(V) → P(V(ω)) = Σ, namely F ↦ F^⊥. He describes σ as the symmetry (half-turn) about the 'polar axis', whose fixed points are the two isotropic lines of e. The margin adds that σ sends the point x, viewed in the dual description, to F^⊥, and that all the isomorphisms (21) are compatible with the action of a group D (dihedral/Weyl-type) by transport of structure.

The next page completes the picture:
- the square PΣ ≅ V* over σ: Σ → Σ ≅ V*/R* (or /C*);
- τσ = antipodal map, where τ is complex conjugation;
- restriction to Σ* = Σ minus the points R_i gives PΣ* ≅ V minus the diagonals Δ_i.

Purpose: a canonical, equivariant linear-algebraic model of the punctured tautological C*-bundle over the genus-0 surface (sphere with marked points R_i). This feeds into describing fundamental groupoids and Teichmüller towers in genus 0 as hyperplane/diagonal complements with group actions.
